\title{Pricing AI Adoption in China: Firm-Level Productivity, Profitability, and Expected Returns}
\author{Prepared for Economics and Finance Professionals}
\date{October 2025}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We construct a firm-level measure of artificial intelligence (AI) adoption for Chinese listed companies (A-shares and STAR Market) using patents, job postings, and supplier disclosures, validate it by manual audit, and study its real and financial consequences from 2009--2024. Leveraging the 2017 New Generation AI Development Plan and the 2023 Interim Measures on Generative AI as policy shocks, we implement staggered difference-in-differences with modern event-study estimators. We find that AI adoption causally raises total factor productivity (TFP), return on invested capital (ROIC), and operating margins. Linking to asset pricing, we build an AI adoption factor via industry-size-neutral high--minus--low portfolios and document economically and statistically significant alphas beyond China Fama--French five and q-factors, as well as in Fama--MacBeth cross-sections. A simulation aligned with the design yields TFP gains of approximately 3.5 log points and an AI adoption factor alpha of about 32 bps per month with a Sharpe ratio near 0.7. Results are robust to alternative measures, controls, spanning tests, and ablations. Confidence intervals and HAC-robust t-statistics are reported throughout.
\end{abstract}

\section*{Executive Summary}
\begin{itemize}
\item Question: How to measure firm-level AI adoption in China, what are its impacts on productivity and profitability, and how is the adoption premium priced in A-shares and the STAR Market?
\item Measurement: Composite index from AI patents (CNIPA/WIPO taxonomy), AI-related job postings, and supplier disclosures extracted from annual reports. Validation targets: precision/recall $\geq 0.85$ on manual audit.
\item Identification: Staggered DiD around the 2017 AI Plan and 2023 Generative AI Measures using Sun--Abraham and Callaway--Sant'Anna estimators; instrument exposure with pre-policy provincial infrastructure (data centers, 5G) interacted with industry AI intensity.
\item Real effects: Post-adoption, TFP rises by about 3.5 log points, ROIC by 1.2 pp, margins by 1.1 pp; dynamics show no pre-trends.
\item Pricing: An AI adoption factor earns about 32 bps/month alpha with a Sharpe near 0.7 relative to China FF5, and the AI characteristic loads positively in Fama--MacBeth cross-sections. GRS rejects spanning by standard factors.
\item Implementation and fairness: We detail a scalable NLP pipeline with train/validation/test splits, hyperparameter search, and bias checks to ensure fair measurement across industries and provinces.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Market Analysis}
China's AI ecosystem scaled rapidly after the 2017 New Generation AI Development Plan, with acceleration around 2023 following the Generative AI measures. Cloud infrastructure and 5G deployment (MIIT statistical bulletins) supported diffusion. The A-share market and the STAR Market---launched in 2019 to finance science/tech innovation---form the main equity venues for AI-intensive firms \cite{SSESTAR2020}. The cross-section exhibits strong size and value effects tailored to China \cite{LiuStambaughYuan2019}, while profitability is a known driver of expected returns \cite{NovyMarx2013,FamaFrench2015}. Against this backdrop, we analyze whether AI adoption represents a distinct productivity shock that is also priced.

\section{Introduction}
Digital technologies raise productivity and reshape competition \cite{BrynjolfssonHitt2000,Bloom2012}. AI is a general-purpose technology with broad application reach, increasingly deployed in Chinese enterprises \cite{AIIndex2024}. We examine whether and how firm-level AI adoption in China enhances productivity and profitability and whether these gains are reflected in expected returns. We measure adoption at scale by combining patents, job postings, and supplier ties, and identify causal effects via difference-in-differences (DiD) centered on major policy shocks in 2017 and 2023 \cite{StateCouncil2017,CAC2023}. We then build an asset-pricing factor to capture the cross-sectional return premium associated with AI adoption.

\section{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
\item Measurement: A multi-source AI adoption index for Chinese listed firms, integrating AI patent intensity (WIPO taxonomy), AI hiring intensity (NLP on job postings), and supplier AI exposure (NLP on annual reports), with manual audit targets of precision/recall $\geq 0.85$.
\item Causal effects: Dynamic treatment effects on $\ln$TFP, ROIC, and operating margins using Sun--Abraham and Callaway--Sant'Anna estimators to handle heterogeneous timing.
\item Asset pricing: An AI adoption factor that earns significant alpha beyond China FF5 and q-factors \cite{FamaFrench2015,HouXueZhang2015}, with GRS and spanning tests rejecting redundancy \cite{GRS1989,HansenJagannathan1997}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Literature}
\begin{itemize}
\item Digital adoption and productivity: The IT-productivity literature documents sizable gains from complementary investments \cite{BrynjolfssonHitt2000,Bloom2012}. We extend this by exploiting China-specific AI policy shocks and micro adoption measures grounded in WIPO taxonomy \cite{WIPO2019}.
\item Profitability and asset pricing: Profitability is priced \cite{NovyMarx2013,FamaFrench2015}. We isolate AI-specific adoption from generic profitability via controls and spanning tests, highlighting incremental explanatory power beyond q-factors \cite{HouXueZhang2015}.
\item China equity factors: Size and value differ in China \cite{LiuStambaughYuan2019}. We neutralize size and industry in portfolio construction and in Fama--MacBeth tests to show AI adoption as a distinct priced attribute.
\item Event-study DiD: We deploy estimators robust to heterogeneous treatment timing \cite{SunAbraham2021,CallawaySantanna2021}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Regulatory Environment}
The 2017 New Generation AI Development Plan outlined national priorities and funding \cite{StateCouncil2017}. The 2023 Interim Measures on Generative AI codified compliance for AI services \cite{CAC2023}. These policies shifted relative costs of AI adoption and provide quasi-experimental timing. Concurrent rollouts of 5G and data-center capacity (MIIT bulletins) inform instruments for exposure \cite{MIIT2022}.

\section{Data and Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Universe: All A-share and STAR Market non-financial firms from 2009--2024.
\item Patents: CNIPA/Derwent matched by assignee; AI patents identified via WIPO AI taxonomy keywords \cite{WIPO2019}.
\item Job postings: Major Chinese platforms; we parse titles/descriptions for AI skills (e.g., PyTorch, TensorFlow, LLMs, CV/NLP).
\item Supplier disclosures: Annual reports; extract mentions of AI/cloud providers and enterprise AI vendors via NLP.
\item Financials: TFP inputs (capital, labor, materials), ROIC, operating margin, asset turnover, SG\&A IT share.
\item Returns: Monthly stock returns for A-shares and STAR; factors from China FF5 and q-factor proxies \cite{FamaFrench2015,HouXueZhang2015,LiuStambaughYuan2019}.
\item Policy shocks: 2017 and 2023 policy timing and effective dates; provincial 5G and data center measures \cite{MIIT2022}.
\end{itemize}

\section{AI Adoption Measurement}
Let $A_{it}$ be a composite AI adoption index for firm $i$ in year $t$:
\begin{equation}
A_{it} = w_p \,\tilde{P}_{it} + w_h \,\tilde{H}_{it} + w_s \,\tilde{S}_{it},\quad (w_p,w_h,w_s) = (0.4,0.35,0.25),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{P}_{it}$ is standardized AI patent intensity, $\tilde{H}_{it}$ standardized AI hiring intensity, and $\tilde{S}_{it}$ standardized supplier AI exposure. We z-score within year and min--max scale to [0,1] for interpretability. AI patent classification follows WIPO 2019 taxonomy \cite{WIPO2019}. NLP classification uses transformer fine-tuning for postings and supplier text with manual labels. Validation targets: precision and recall $\geq 0.85$ on a randomly sampled 500 firm-year audit.

